1
00:00:02,179 --> 00:00:04,534
Now before we write code, let's understand

2
00:00:04,582 --> 00:00:06,810
how balancing should work in theory.

3
00:00:07,483 --> 00:00:08,300
There are 4

4
00:00:08,829 --> 00:00:12,162
ways of balancing out an AVL tree, and with

5
00:00:12,194 --> 00:00:14,341
that I don't mean 4 different approaches,

6
00:00:14,854 --> 00:00:19,148
but 4 ways of fixing an unbalanced binary

7
00:00:19,180 --> 00:00:23,090
search tree to make it a valid AVL tree. And

8
00:00:23,266 --> 00:00:25,910
which way you pick depends on how your tree

9
00:00:25,910 --> 00:00:26,567
is structured.

10
00:00:27,336 --> 00:00:30,044
For example, you might need a left rotation.

11
00:00:30,716 --> 00:00:32,687
Now what is that and when do you need it?

12
00:00:33,584 --> 00:00:36,644
Consider this binary search tree. We have a

13
00:00:36,644 --> 00:00:39,271
value of 1 at the top, then we have a value

14
00:00:39,271 --> 00:00:41,450
of 2 on the right branch of that, and a

15
00:00:41,450 --> 00:00:43,645
value of 3 on the right branch of 2.

16
00:00:44,414 --> 00:00:46,256
Now this is an unbalanced tree, so we need

17
00:00:46,256 --> 00:00:46,881
to fix it.

18
00:00:47,458 --> 00:00:50,982
And we do fix it by rotating left. And you

19
00:00:50,982 --> 00:00:53,802
can really think of our rotation here. We

20
00:00:53,866 --> 00:00:57,230
basically move the node with value 2 to the

21
00:00:57,230 --> 00:01:00,132
place where 1 is But we don't swap the 2,

22
00:01:00,437 --> 00:01:03,117
but we simply make 1 a child of 2 now.

23
00:01:04,032 --> 00:01:06,295
So that in the end, we end up with this. It

24
00:01:06,295 --> 00:01:09,826
is as if we rotated this entire unbalanced

25
00:01:09,826 --> 00:01:11,993
tree to the left, if you think of it like

26
00:01:11,993 --> 00:01:12,121
this.

27
00:01:12,795 --> 00:01:15,588
It still has the same sequence, 1, 2, 3,

28
00:01:16,165 --> 00:01:18,493
but it simply was rotated to the left.

29
00:01:19,199 --> 00:01:22,088
This kind of fix is needed if we have a

30
00:01:22,088 --> 00:01:25,763
right-heavy tree. So if our right branch is

31
00:01:25,955 --> 00:01:28,974
unbalanced to the left branch. So to say, as

32
00:01:28,974 --> 00:01:29,820
it is the case here.

33
00:01:32,350 --> 00:01:34,601
Obviously, we therefore also have situations

34
00:01:35,019 --> 00:01:37,818
where we need a right rotation, for example

35
00:01:37,915 --> 00:01:40,665
on this binary search tree. Here it's

36
00:01:40,665 --> 00:01:43,872
basically the opposite. We have 3 as a first

37
00:01:43,872 --> 00:01:46,433
value, as a root node, and therefore on the

38
00:01:46,514 --> 00:01:50,234
left node, the left child of 3 is 2 and the

39
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:51,878
left child of 2 is 1.

40
00:01:52,608 --> 00:01:55,031
Again, this is unbalanced. Now just in the

41
00:01:55,047 --> 00:01:55,976
opposite direction.

42
00:01:56,569 --> 00:01:58,796
And the fix here would be a right rotation

43
00:01:58,892 --> 00:02:01,744
to end up with this kind of AVL tree. It's

44
00:02:01,792 --> 00:02:04,708
the same AVL tree as before, but the fix is

45
00:02:04,837 --> 00:02:07,048
different because the starting tree is

46
00:02:07,144 --> 00:02:09,563
different. And I hope it's clear when we

47
00:02:09,563 --> 00:02:12,496
need what. It should be fairly obvious with

48
00:02:12,608 --> 00:02:14,082
those example trees here.

49
00:02:15,396 --> 00:02:17,062
Now these are the 2 easy fixes.

50
00:02:17,655 --> 00:02:20,731
We can also end up with trees where one

51
00:02:20,843 --> 00:02:22,125
rotation is not enough.

52
00:02:22,702 --> 00:02:25,526
We also might need a left and then a right

53
00:02:25,574 --> 00:02:26,103
rotation.

54
00:02:26,632 --> 00:02:27,643
And here's an example.

55
00:02:28,237 --> 00:02:30,691
We start with a binary search tree which

56
00:02:30,787 --> 00:02:34,268
looks like this. Still 3 values, but now we

57
00:02:34,316 --> 00:02:38,150
have 3 as a topmost value, then on its left

58
00:02:38,246 --> 00:02:40,877
branch as a left child we have 1,

59
00:02:41,455 --> 00:02:43,572
and as a right child to 1 we have 2.

60
00:02:44,502 --> 00:02:46,973
Now to fix this and turn this into an AVL

61
00:02:47,085 --> 00:02:49,411
tree, one rotation will not be enough.

62
00:02:50,172 --> 00:02:52,389
Instead, what we'll need to do is we'll need

63
00:02:52,421 --> 00:02:54,718
to start with a left rotation

64
00:02:55,409 --> 00:02:57,578
so that we end up with this kind of tree,

65
00:02:59,474 --> 00:03:01,831
and then we need another right rotation to

66
00:03:01,831 --> 00:03:03,463
turn this into an AVL tree.

67
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:06,356
And this is how we would fix this binary

68
00:03:06,356 --> 00:03:08,780
search tree to turn it into a balanced tree.

69
00:03:11,232 --> 00:03:13,560
Here, there simply is no way of fixing this

70
00:03:13,577 --> 00:03:14,854
with just one rotation.

71
00:03:15,818 --> 00:03:17,602
Now obviously, we therefore also have a

72
00:03:17,602 --> 00:03:18,984
right-left rotation.

73
00:03:19,594 --> 00:03:21,779
Here is the input binary search tree where

74
00:03:21,779 --> 00:03:23,884
we might need one, 1, 3, 2.

75
00:03:24,495 --> 00:03:27,050
And again, we start with a rotation to the

76
00:03:27,066 --> 00:03:29,476
right in this case to end up with this tree,

77
00:03:29,492 --> 00:03:32,128
which is still unbalanced. So now we make a

78
00:03:32,128 --> 00:03:35,309
left rotation and we have our balanced AVL

79
00:03:35,438 --> 00:03:35,679
tree.

80
00:03:36,241 --> 00:03:38,877
So you see, for these basic examples, we

81
00:03:39,037 --> 00:03:41,366
always end up with the same result. But we

82
00:03:41,366 --> 00:03:43,450
have different input trees and therefore

83
00:03:43,546 --> 00:03:44,860
different ways of getting there.

84
00:03:45,533 --> 00:03:48,515
And this is what AVL trees are about. They

85
00:03:48,659 --> 00:03:50,246
are binary search trees,

86
00:03:50,903 --> 00:03:53,371
but binary search trees with a built-in

87
00:03:53,547 --> 00:03:56,496
optimization step that ensures that we'll

88
00:03:56,528 --> 00:03:58,820
never have the worst-case time complexity

89
00:03:59,173 --> 00:04:01,866
because we balance the tree every time a new

90
00:04:01,962 --> 00:04:02,875
value is added.
